UI-P2 review — declarative extension UI bridge

Scope
- Adds the standard SDK UI registration/action API, Node host transport, Rust attribution/validation/catalog and bounded IPC projection.
- Migrates extensions.json from v1 to v2 with a complete backup, unknown-field preservation and inert legacy UI diagnostics.
- Does not render third-party UI yet; rendering begins in UI-P3.

Security and ownership
- HostIdentity plus host generation is the identity authority; extension-supplied IDs cannot select another owner.
- Official shared-host plugins remain distinct; third-party hosts can publish only their own ID.
- Contributions, trees, text, locales, fields, options, actions, payloads, results, diagnostics, tombstones and global snapshots are bounded and revalidated in Rust.
- UI failure is isolated from valid tools; visible action failures stay generic while internal reasons are bounded and sanitized.
- Disable/stop/restart retires the catalog generation; late action responses cannot mutate a reloaded catalog.

Review corrections applied before commit
- Measure bytes after ID normalization.
- Enforce dynamic actions per extension, not per contribution.
- Bound Rust depth/node traversal before recursion.
- Reject stale same-host action results by catalog revision.
- Deduplicate UI diagnostics while validating every received code.
- Keep advanced UI inert until UI-P5 and omit standard entry instead of serializing null.
- Extend generated contract authorities for modes, contribution types, primitives and theme bases.
- Update the complete-capability assertion to include ui.

